 ## Background
-Regularization in NeRFs originated from the need to resolve the shape-radiance ambiguity, where different combinations of density and color can produce the same image. Early methods like RegNeRF [S1] introduced geometric smoothness and normalizing flow priors to constrain unobserved regions. The integration of diffusion models, such as in Diffusion-regularized NeRF [S1, S2], marked a shift towards using learned generative priors from large synthetic datasets to guide geometry. More recently, semantic consistency using CLIP [S1, S3] and structural similarity metrics like S3IM [S1] have been adopted to improve perceptual quality and semantic alignment in sparse-view and text-guided scenarios.
+The need for regularization in NeRFs arose from the observation that standard photometric reconstruction alone leads to ambiguous solutions, particularly in sparse-view settings, resulting in "floaters" (semi-transparent artifacts in empty space) and poor generalization [S1, 8582-regnerf.md]. Early methods like RegNeRF introduced geometric smoothness and generative color priors to constrain unobserved regions [S1]. Subsequent work expanded this to include diffusion-based priors for better geometric plausibility [S1], semantic consistency via CLIP for text-guided editing [S1, S3], and multi-view consistency to solve the Janus problem in 3D generation [S1]. The field has evolved from simple depth smoothness constraints to complex, multi-modal regularization strategies involving diffusion models, vision-language models, and physically-based rendering constraints [S1, S2, S5].
 
